hyc@hanauma.jpl.nasa.gov (Howard Chu) writes:

>In article <1992Dec16.064209.8367@eskimo.com> gregs@eskimo.com (Greg Sumner) writes:
>>I've been pondering this idea too, and I came up with the same problem.
>>You'd have a bunch of players starting at the same place.  It would end up
>>being a race through the levels to see who could get to the good stuff
>>first.  Who wants to join into a game where the first 15 levels are
>>already looted anyway?

- You cannot take everything with you, so players could find things others
  had left behind. This means that players should be able to hide stuff,
  and other players should be able to find hidden stuff. This could mean
  things like scrolls and spellbooks of hiding and finding, invisible boxes
  and bags, ability to make hidden doors, etc.
- If everybody is digging around, (parts of) dungeons should have a 
  possibility of collapsing. Imagine the idea of getting trapped when part
  of the dungeon collapses because some drunk dwarf is digging around? 
  Especially when you don't have a pickaxe? 
- Let players be able to steal from eachother. It would be nice if a player
  could hit another player unconcious without killing. Killing other players
  could have some bad consequences.
- I'm not too fond of the idea of generating lots of stuff. Maybe there
  should be sub-quests, like: kill a dragon, cut off some scales, bring 
  them to an armour-shop, where you pay the shop-owner to make a dragon-
  cale mail out of it.
  
I'll stop now. I'm getting carried away.

